"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,610\nThe Learning Brain. Series 8: Assessment That Teaches\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,314\nEpisode 9\n\n3\n00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:09,605\nMastery and retakes\n\n4\n00:00:12,405 --> 00:00:16,611\nMost courses move on whether students have mastered a topic or not.\n\n5\n00:00:17,142 --> 00:00:18,444\nThe test is taken,\n\n6\n00:00:18,621 --> 00:00:20,157\nthe grade is recorded,\n\n7\n00:00:20,218 --> 00:00:21,786\nand the next unit begins.\n\n8\n00:00:22,208 --> 00:00:24,784\nWhat if students had to reach a standard first,\n\n9\n00:00:24,933 --> 00:00:26,822\nwith another try if they missed it?\n\n10\n00:00:26,992 --> 00:00:29,659\nThat is the idea behind mastery learning.\n\n11\n00:00:30,093 --> 00:00:33,158\nStudents work toward a clear standard on each unit,\n\n12\n00:00:33,398 --> 00:00:35,278\nget feedback and extra help,\n\n13\n00:00:35,509 --> 00:00:37,922\nand retake an assessment until they meet it.\n\n14\n00:00:38,092 --> 00:00:40,866\nIn 1990, Chen-Lin Kulik,\n\n15\n00:00:41,210 --> 00:00:46,107\nJames Kulik and Robert Bangert-Drowns reviewed one hundred eight controlled studies,\n\n16\n00:00:46,363 --> 00:00:50,602\nin colleges, high schools and the upper elementary grades.\n\n17\n00:00:51,108 --> 00:00:57,207\nMastery programs raised exam scores by about half a standard deviation on average.\n\n18\n00:00:57,377 --> 00:01:00,103\nThe benefit was larger for weaker students.\n\n19\n00:01:00,433 --> 00:01:02,376\nMastery learning narrowed the gap,\n\n20\n00:01:02,670 --> 00:01:04,018\nrather than widening it.\n\n21\n00:01:04,188 --> 00:01:06,761\nMastery programs also had moderate,\n\n22\n00:01:06,939 --> 00:01:11,563\npositive effects on how students felt about the subject and the teaching.\n\n23\n00:01:11,733 --> 00:01:12,753\nThere is a cost.\n\n24\n00:01:13,136 --> 00:01:17,355\nSelf paced mastery programs sometimes lowered course completion,\n\n25\n00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:19,075\nas students fell behind.\n\n26\n00:01:19,601 --> 00:01:23,873\nAnd mastery programs can increase the time students spend on instruction.\n\n27\n00:01:24,318 --> 00:01:26,870\nRetakes take time for instructors too.\n\n28\n00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:28,495\nSo design matters.\n\n29\n00:01:28,882 --> 00:01:32,655\nRetakes work best when they require new learning between attempts,\n\n30\n00:01:32,924 --> 00:01:34,643\nuse new versions of questions,\n\n31\n00:01:34,663 --> 00:01:36,284\nand fit a clear schedule.\n\n32\n00:01:36,454 --> 00:01:37,218\nFor teaching.\n\n33\n00:01:37,619 --> 00:01:40,198\nChoose the few outcomes that truly matter,\n\n34\n00:01:40,218 --> 00:01:42,138\nand allow a retake on those.\n\n35\n00:01:42,466 --> 00:01:45,995\nRequire a short correction or study step before a retake.\n\n36\n00:01:46,461 --> 00:01:50,454\nUse fresh questions, and set deadlines so students keep pace.\n\n37\n00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:55,226\nOne tip to share with students: use a retake to fix what you missed.\n\n38\n00:01:55,396 --> 00:01:56,668\nExpect mastery.\n\n39\n00:01:56,976 --> 00:02:00,207\nThen give students the time and the second chance to reach it.\n\n"